Accreditation Theory
A Mathematical Framework for Quantifying Information Credibility in Human and Multi‑Planetary Civilizations

Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Meta AI

Abstract

Contemporary information systems lack a rigorous mathematical measure of credibility. Popularity, engagement, institutional authority, and binary fact‑checking dominate the evaluation of knowledge, yet none quantifies the degree to which available evidence justifies belief in a proposition. This absence is tolerable for societies optimised for information velocity; for future autonomous systems and interplanetary civilisations where communication delays and distributed governance are the norm, it becomes an existential vulnerability.

This thesis proposes Accreditation Theory, a mathematical framework that cleanly separates truth—an objective property of reality that may remain unknown—from accreditation—the time‑varying, evidence‑based confidence that it is rational to assign to a claim. The theory models information as a dynamic graph of propositions, sources, evidence, and verification processes, and defines the Accreditation Index $A_i(t)$, a continuous quantity derived from belief functions that explicitly represents ignorance and conflict.

The framework unfolds in three layers. First, we construct the index from first principles using Dempster–Shafer belief functions, regularised uncertainty measures, and a principled choice of aggregation operators dictated by an impossibility theorem. Second, we embed this index in a network propagation model governed by a primitive stochastic matrix; we prove convergence to consensus via Perron–Frobenius theory and bound the rate by the spectral gap, while extending the analysis to delayed and correlated evidence through Lyapunov–Krasovskii stability and the Condorcet Jury Theorem. Third, we recast the entire theory in the language of cellular sheaves, showing that global consistency is not required for multi‑planetary systems—only local consistency on shared edges—and that the obstruction to full consensus is measured by the cohomology group $H^1(G;\mathcal{F})$.

The final chapter formalises six open research directions as precise mathematical conjectures, each accompanied by a proof strategy grounded in stochastic approximation, information geometry, and distributed optimisation. These include an extension of the aggregation impossibility to non‑Bayesian belief functions, two‑timescale convergence of adaptive accreditation, certified adversarial robustness via randomised smoothing, thermodynamic speed limits for path‑dependent credibility, directed sheaf Laplacians, and differentially private sheaf‑ADMM algorithms.

By elevating credibility from a matter of institutional trust to a measurable, evolving mathematical quantity, Accreditation Theory provides a universal foundation for information integrity on Earth and for the autonomous civilisations that will one day extend beyond it.

