Chapter 1: Introduction

1.1 The Credibility Paradox

Human civilisation invests enormous effort in accrediting the entities that produce and disseminate knowledge. Universities grant degrees, professional bodies issue licences, financial regulators audit institutions, and peer review vets scientific manuscripts. These systems are slow, expensive, and imperfect, but they represent a societal consensus that credibility must be earned and periodically re‑examined. Yet the very product that these accredited entities deliver—information—is evaluated by no comparable mathematical standard. A social media post, a news headline, a scientific preprint, and a market rumour are all measured primarily by their velocity: how many shares, impressions, or citations they accumulate before any systematic verification can take place.

This asymmetry is not accidental. Until recently, the bottleneck in the information pipeline was production; today, it is verification. Algorithms optimised for engagement amplify content before its credibility can be assessed, and the result is a public sphere that often rewards interesting falsehoods over boring truths. The problem is not that we lack fact‑checkers; it is that we lack a mathematical language in which credibility can be stated, propagated, and updated with the same precision as a physical measurement.

1.2 Beyond Binary Fact‑Checking

The dominant responses to the credibility crisis have been institutional—fact‑checking organisations, content moderation, platform curation—and, in academia, the development of computational tools for fake news detection, stance classification, and rumour tracking. These approaches share a common limitation: they are fundamentally binary. A claim is labelled “true” or “false,” a source “reliable” or “unreliable.” Reality, however, is rarely binary.

Many of the most consequential propositions facing society—the efficacy of a new medical treatment, the trajectory of climate change, the integrity of an election—are characterised by deep uncertainty and conflicting evidence. At any given moment, the available data may support a range of plausible conclusions, and the rational stance is one of graded confidence rather than absolute belief or disbelief. Furthermore, even when a proposition will eventually be resolved as true or false, that resolution may arrive years after the decision it must inform.

This gap between binary judgement and graded evidence is not merely philosophical; it has practical consequences. Financial markets, for instance, do not wait for certainty; they price assets continuously on the basis of partial information. Autonomous AI agents, whether operating in a terrestrial logistics network or a Martian habitat, cannot pause to await a definitive fact‑check; they must act on the best available evidence, with an explicit representation of how much they do not know. What is missing is a framework that quantifies the current state of justification for any claim, in a way that can be updated as new evidence arrives and that can propagate through networks of interrelated propositions.

1.3 The Accreditation Perspective

This thesis introduces Accreditation Theory to fill that gap. The central move is a conceptual one: we abandon the attempt to determine whether a statement is true and ask instead the more tractable question:

Given all currently available evidence, what is the mathematically justified confidence that society should assign to this information?

We call this justified confidence the accreditation of the proposition, and we denote it $A_i(t)$. Accreditation is not a probability of truth in the metaphysical sense—truth is an objective property of reality that may remain unknown indefinitely—but a formal measure of the evidential support at time $t$. It is an epistemic, not an ontic, quantity. This separation of accreditation from truth is the foundational axiom of the theory and the key to its applicability under irreducible uncertainty.

The Accreditation Index $A_i(t)$ is defined on a continuous scale from $0$ (complete rejection) to $1$ (maximal support), with explicit provision for ignorance. It is not a scalar belief degree but a functional of a richer mathematical object: a Dempster–Shafer belief function, which separately quantifies the mass of evidence for a proposition, the mass of evidence against it, and the mass of evidence that is uncommitted. This allows the index to distinguish between a lack of evidence (high ignorance) and conflicting evidence (high conflict), a distinction that binary or purely probabilistic models collapse.

Accreditation is not static; it evolves as new evidence arrives, as sources are evaluated, and as the logical and semantic connections between propositions transmit credibility through the information ecosystem. The ecosystem itself is modelled as a directed, time‑varying graph whose vertices represent propositions, sources, evidence items, observers, and verifiers. The dynamics of accreditation on this graph are governed by a combination of Bayesian (or belief‑function) updating at the local level and a linear consensus process at the network level. The result is a dynamical system in which the credibility of every proposition is a measurable, evolving quantity that can be computed, predicted, and bounded.

1.4 Why Mathematics?

One might reasonably ask: why does credibility need a mathematical theory at all? Are not the existing social and institutional mechanisms sufficient? Three trends suggest otherwise.

First, the scale of information production has exceeded the capacity of human‑mediated verification. The volume of digital content doubles every few years; manual fact‑checking can address only a tiny fraction of it. Any solution that does not scale computationally is, by definition, a partial solution.

Second, autonomous AI agents are becoming primary consumers and producers of information. These agents, whether in high‑frequency trading, robotic surgery, or interplanetary navigation, require formal, computable representations of uncertainty. An AI cannot appeal to “institutional reputation” in the way a human can; it needs a numerical prior and an update rule.

Third, and most critically, humanity is on the threshold of becoming a multi‑planetary species. The communication delay between Earth and Mars varies between 4 and 24 minutes; for the outer planets, it is measured in hours. Centralised fact‑checking, even if it were desirable, is physically impossible under such latency. Each settlement will need the ability to evaluate information locally while maintaining consistency with the broader human knowledge base. A mathematical accreditation framework provides the only conceivable foundation for such an autonomous yet coherent information infrastructure.

These considerations make clear that the problem of credibility is not merely a social or psychological one. It is a problem of information theory, network science, and statistical inference—a problem that admits, and indeed demands, a rigorous mathematical treatment.

1.5 Contributions of This Thesis

This thesis develops Accreditation Theory from first principles to open research frontiers. The principal contributions are:

1. Axiomatic foundation. We define the Accreditation Index as a functional of belief functions, motivated by an impossibility theorem that dictates the choice of aggregation operators. We prove that no simultaneous satisfaction of coherent marginalisation and external Bayesianity exists, and derive the canonical cross‑source pool as the unique admissible operator for asynchronous evidence arrival.
2. Network propagation and consensus. We model the information ecosystem as a dynamic graph and prove that accreditation, when propagated via a primitive stochastic matrix, converges to a global consensus. The convergence rate is explicitly bounded by the spectral gap, connecting credibility dynamics to the structural properties of the information network.
3. Multi‑agent independence. We ground the theory in modern extensions of the Condorcet Jury Theorem, showing that correlated evidence contributes less to accreditation than genuinely independent verification, and incorporating this insight into source weighting.
4. Temporal stability and delay resilience. We formulate a delay differential equation for accreditation and apply Lyapunov‑Krasovskii functionals to derive explicit stability thresholds for interplanetary communication delays.
5. Sheaf‑theoretic reformulation. We recast accreditation as a cochain on a cellular sheaf over the information graph. This reveals that global consensus is not necessary for multi‑planetary systems; local consistency on shared edges is sufficient. The cohomology group $H^1(G;\mathcal{F})$ is identified as the mathematical obstruction to full agreement.
6. Formalised future programme (Chapter 12). We articulate six open conjectures—extending aggregation impossibility to non‑Bayesian belief functions, proving two‑timescale convergence of adaptive dynamics, certifying adversarial robustness, establishing thermodynamic speed limits for path‑dependent accreditation, extending sheaf theory to directed and time‑varying graphs, and designing differentially private distributed algorithms—each accompanied by a precise proof strategy and a survey of the required mathematical background.

1.6 Structure of the Thesis

Chapter 2 provides the mathematical foundations: measure‑theoretic probability, belief functions, spectral graph theory, consensus dynamics, stochastic approximation, sheaf theory, and information geometry. This chapter may be skipped by readers familiar with these topics but is essential for the precise formulation of later results.

Chapters 3–7 construct the core of Accreditation Theory: the fundamental principle, the definition of the Accreditation Index, the information network graph, the local update rules (Bayesian and belief‑function), and the fusion of evidence with explicit ignorance representation.

Chapters 8–11 develop the dynamical aspects: network propagation with convergence proofs, multi‑agent verification under correlation, temporal stability under delay, and Byzantine‑resilient architectures for multi‑planetary systems.

Chapter 12 is the capstone of the thesis. It formalises six open research directions as rigorous mathematical problems, each with a definition, a theorem (proved where possible, otherwise stated as a conjecture), and a detailed proof strategy. This chapter is designed to serve as a research roadmap for the mathematical community and as a demonstration of the depth and coherence of the Accreditation Theory programme.

Chapter 13 concludes.

1.7 A Note on Style

Throughout this thesis, we adopt the convention that theorems are results for which a complete proof is provided (or cited from the established literature), conjectures are stated precisely with an outline of the expected proof strategy and a clear identification of the missing steps, and definitions are the building blocks of the formal language. This tripartite distinction is intended to maintain intellectual honesty: Accreditation Theory is a young framework, and it is more valuable to state open problems clearly than to pretend they are solved. The hope is that this thesis will be read not as the final word but as the opening of a new mathematical conversation.

Chapter 2: Mathematical Foundations

This chapter provides the rigorous definitions and theorems that underpin the entire framework. All subsequent results depend on the constructions given here.

2.1 Probability and Bayesian Inference

Let (\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P}) be a probability space. For any H\in\mathcal{F} with \mathbb{P}(H)>0 and E\in\mathcal{F} with \mathbb{P}(E)>0, the conditional probability of H given E is

\mathbb{P}(H\mid E)=\frac{\mathbb{P}(E\cap H)}{\mathbb{P}(E)}.

In a parametric setting, a prior density \pi(\theta) on a parameter space \Theta is combined with a likelihood f(x\mid\theta) via Bayes’ theorem to obtain the posterior \pi(\theta\mid x)\propto f(x\mid\theta)\pi(\theta).

A Bayesian network over variables X_1,\dots,X_n is a directed acyclic graph (DAG) whose joint distribution factorises as

p(x_1,\dots,x_n)=\prod_{i=1}^n p(x_i\mid \mathrm{pa}(x_i)),

where \mathrm{pa}(x_i) are the parents of X_i. The sum‑product algorithm (belief propagation) computes exact marginal distributions on trees; for general graphs, loopy belief propagation or variational inference (Wainwright & Jordan 2008) is used.

2.2 Belief Functions and Imprecise Probabilities

Let \Omega be a finite set, the frame of discernment. A basic probability assignment (bpa) is a function m:2^\Omega\to[0,1] satisfying m(\emptyset)=0 and \sum_{A\subseteq\Omega}m(A)=1. The subsets A with m(A)>0 are called focal elements.

Definition 2.1 (Belief and Plausibility). For a bpa m, the belief function \Bel:2^\Omega\to[0,1] and the plausibility function \Pl are

\Bel(A)=\sum_{B\subseteq A} m(B),\qquad 
\Pl(A)=\sum_{B\cap A\neq\emptyset} m(B) = 1 - \Bel(\overline{A}).

Definition 2.2 (Commonality). The commonality function Q:2^\Omega\to[0,1] is

Q(A)=\sum_{B\supseteq A} m(B).

It satisfies Q(\emptyset)=1, and m can be recovered by Möbius inversion.

Definition 2.3 (Dempster’s rule of combination). For two bpas m_1,m_2 over the same frame \Omega, their orthogonal sum is

(m_1\oplus m_2)(A)=\frac{1}{1-K}\sum_{B\cap C=A}m_1(B)m_2(C),\qquad 
K=\sum_{B\cap C=\emptyset}m_1(B)m_2(C).

If K=1, the sources are totally conflicting and the rule is undefined; alternative combination rules (Yager, Dubois–Prade, cautious rule) exist for this case.

A bpa is Bayesian if it assigns positive mass only to singletons, i.e. it is a probability measure.

Conditioning. We adopt the Fagin–Halpern conditional (Fagin & Halpern 1991) as the canonical update in the belief‑function setting. For a non‑empty conditioning event B\subseteq\Omega, the conditional belief is

\Bel(A\mid B)=\frac{\Bel(A\cap B)}{\Bel(A\cap B)+\Pl(\overline{A}\cap B)}.

When all masses are on singletons, this reduces to standard Bayesian conditioning. This is the update rule used throughout the thesis.

2.3 Spectral Graph Theory and Consensus

For a directed graph G=(V,E) with adjacency matrix \mathbf{A}, let \mathbf{D} be the diagonal out‑degree matrix. The Laplacian is \mathbf{L}=\mathbf{D}-\mathbf{A}. For an undirected graph, \mathbf{L} is symmetric positive semidefinite; its smallest eigenvalue is 0 with multiplicity equal to the number of connected components.

A square matrix \mathbf{W}\in\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}^{n\times n} is row‑stochastic if \sum_j W_{ij}=1 for all i. It is primitive if it is non‑negative and some power \mathbf{W}^k has all entries strictly positive.

Theorem 2.1 (Perron–Frobenius). If \mathbf{W} is a primitive row‑stochastic matrix, then it has a simple eigenvalue \lambda_1=1 and all other eigenvalues satisfy |\lambda_j|<1. There exists a unique positive left eigenvector \mathbf{v} with \mathbf{v}^\top\mathbf{W}=\mathbf{v}^\top and \sum_i v_i=1.

DeGroot consensus model. For opinions \mathbf{x}(t)\in\mathbb{R}^n, the dynamics \mathbf{x}(t+1)=\mathbf{W}\mathbf{x}(t) with primitive \mathbf{W} converge to the consensus \bar{x}\mathbf{1} where \bar{x}=\mathbf{v}^\top\mathbf{x}(0). The rate of convergence is governed by the second largest eigenvalue modulus |\lambda_2|.

2.4 Stochastic Approximation

The Robbins–Monro algorithm iterates

\theta_{t+1}=\theta_t + a_t H(\theta_t,X_t),

with step sizes satisfying \sum a_t=\infty, \sum a_t^2<\infty. Under mild conditions (Kushner & Yin 2003), \theta_t converges almost surely to a root of the mean field \overline{H}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}[H(\theta,X)], provided the ODE \dot\theta=\overline{H}(\theta) has a unique globally asymptotically stable equilibrium.

Two‑timescale stochastic approximation (Borkar 1997). Consider

\theta_t = \theta_{t-1}+a_t f(\theta_{t-1},\phi_{t-1}),\qquad
\phi_t = \phi_{t-1}+b_t g(\theta_{t-1},\phi_{t-1}),

with b_t/a_t\to0. The fast process \phi sees \theta as quasi‑static; the slow process \theta sees \phi as equilibrated. Under Lipschitz and stability conditions, \theta_t converges almost surely.

2.5 Cellular Sheaves

A cellular sheaf \mathcal{F} over a directed graph G=(V,E) assigns a finite‑dimensional real vector space \mathcal{F}(v) to each vertex and \mathcal{F}(e) to each edge. For every incident vertex‑edge pair v\triangleleft e, there is a linear restriction map \mathcal{F}_{v\triangleleft e}:\mathcal{F}(v)\to\mathcal{F}(e).

A cochain x\in C^0(G;\mathcal{F}) is a choice x_v\in\mathcal{F}(v) for each vertex. The coboundary operator \delta:C^0(G;\mathcal{F})\to C^1(G;\mathcal{F}) is defined, for an edge e from u to v, by

(\delta x)_e = \mathcal{F}_{v\triangleleft e}(x_v) - \mathcal{F}_{u\triangleleft e}(x_u).

The sheaf Laplacian L_{\mathcal{F}}:C^0(G;\mathcal{F})\to C^0(G;\mathcal{F}) is L_{\mathcal{F}}=\delta^*\delta, where the adjoint is taken with respect to the standard inner products. For each vertex v,

(L_{\mathcal{F}}x)_v = \sum_{e\ni v} \mathcal{F}_{v\triangleleft e}^*\big(\mathcal{F}_{v\triangleleft e}(x_v)-\mathcal{F}_{w(e)\triangleleft e}(x_{w(e)})\big),

where w(e) is the other endpoint of e.

Theorem 2.2 (Hansen & Ghrist 2021). For an undirected graph (or a directed graph with the above symmetric Laplacian), L_{\mathcal{F}} is symmetric positive semidefinite. The continuous‑time dynamics \dot x=-L_{\mathcal{F}}x converge to \ker L_{\mathcal{F}}, the space of harmonic cochains, i.e. cochains satisfying (\delta x)_e=0 for all edges. The cohomology group H^1(G;\mathcal{F})\cong \ker L_{\mathcal{F}} measures the obstruction to a global consensus.

This sheaf framework will be central to our multi‑planetary consistency analysis.

2.6 Information Geometry and Speed Limits

For a time‑dependent probability density p_t over some state space, the temporal Fisher information is I_F(t)=\mathbb{E}_{p_t}[(\partial_t\ln p_t)^2].

Theorem 2.3 (Ito & Dechant 2020). For any observable O with finite variance \sigma_O^2(t) under p_t,

\left|\frac{d}{dt}\mathbb{E}_{p_t}[O]\right|\le \sigma_O(t)\sqrt{I_F(t)}.

This is a thermodynamic speed limit; it bounds the rate of change of any observable by the product of its fluctuations and the Fisher information of the driving process.

2.7 Byzantine Fault Tolerance and Differential Privacy

A distributed system of N nodes can tolerate up to f Byzantine (arbitrarily faulty) nodes if N\ge 3f+1 (Lamport et al. 1982). Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerant (PBFT) protocols achieve consensus under this condition.

Differential privacy (Dwork & Roth 2014). A randomised mechanism \mathcal{M} is \epsilon-differentially private (\epsilon-DP) if for any two neighbouring datasets D,D' and any measurable set of outputs S,

\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{M}(D)\in S)\le e^\epsilon\,\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{M}(D')\in S).

The Laplace mechanism adds \mathrm{Lap}(\Delta/\epsilon) noise to a query, where \Delta is the \ell_1-sensitivity, and guarantees pure \epsilon-DP. Basic composition over K queries yields K\epsilon-DP.

