Chapter 4: The Accreditation Index

Let \Omega be a fixed, finite frame of discernment representing all relevant possibilities. For a proposition M_i, its truth set is T_i\subseteq\Omega. At any time t, the available evidence induces a bpa m_i(\cdot;t) over \Omega.

Definition 4.1 (Accreditation Index). The accreditation index A_i(t)\in[0,1] is the functional

A_i(t)=\Phi\big(\Bel_i(T_i;t),\Pl_i(T_i;t),U_{\!\epsilon}(m_i(\cdot;t))\big),

where \Bel_i,\Pl_i are computed from m_i(\cdot;t), and U_{\!\epsilon} is a regularised uncertainty measure. The map \Phi:\mathbb{R}^3\to[0,1] is taken as the canonical form

\Phi(B,P,U)=\frac{B+P}{2}(1-U),

with the requirement that U_{\!\epsilon} is globally Lipschitz. A concrete choice is

U_{\!\epsilon}(m)= -\sum_{A\subseteq\Omega} m(A)\log\frac{m(A)+\epsilon}{2^{|A|}-1+\epsilon},

for a small \epsilon>0. This regularised Deng entropy is Lipschitz on the simplex and reduces to the standard Deng entropy as \epsilon\to0. The normalised value \widehat{U}=U_{\!\epsilon}/\log_2(2^{|\Omega|}-1) lies in [0,1].

The index is monotone in \Bel,\Pl and decreasing in uncertainty. When evidence is conclusive, \Bel=\Pl=1 and U=0 give A=1; when evidence is fully contradictory, both \Bel and \Pl can be small.

