Chapter 5: Information Network Graph

The information ecosystem is modelled as a directed, time‑varying graph

G(t)=(V(t),E(t)),

where V(t) comprises nodes of types: proposition, source, evidence, observer, verifier. Directed edges e=(v,u) carry a type \tau(e)\in\{\text{cites},\text{observes},\text{implies},\text{contradicts},\text{validates},\dots\} and a confidence weight c_e\in[0,1] indicating the strength of the relation. The graph evolves as new information appears.

---

Chapter 6: Bayesian Updating on the Graph

When a proposition M_i is directly linked to evidence E (interpreted as a conditioning event on the state \Omega), the bpa is updated using the Fagin–Halpern conditional:

\Bel_i(T_i\mid E)=\frac{\Bel_i(T_i\cap E)}{\Bel_i(T_i\cap E)+\Pl_i(\overline{T}_i\cap E)}.

If the bpa is Bayesian, this collapses to Bayes’ rule. For a single source that receives sequential evidence items, Dempster’s rule (Definition 2.3) provides a robust fusion method that preserves the mass distribution, whereas the Fagin–Halpern conditional is used for conditioning on observed evidence. This dual use is consistent: Dempster’s rule combines independent evidence, while conditional updating shifts the frame after an event is known.

For networks of propositions, belief propagation (Pearl 1988) on the underlying Bayesian network (or its credal extension) computes the marginal beliefs efficiently when the graph is a polytree; otherwise, loopy belief propagation or variational methods approximate the inference.

---

Chapter 7: Evidence Fusion and Ignorance Representation

Independent evidence items are fused through Dempster’s rule. The resulting bpa explicitly models ignorance as the gap \Pl-\Bel. The accreditation index inherits this ignorance directly, because it depends on \Bel,\Pl, and the entropy of the bpa. When conflict is high, the cautious rule of the Transferable Belief Model (Smets 1993) or the weighted mixing of commonalities can be used, but the core principle remains: the index never forces a binary conclusion when evidence is incomplete.

