Chapter 8: Network Propagation and Accreditation Dynamics

We now define the propagation of accreditation across the information graph.

Definition 8.1 (Propagation matrix). At time t, for each directed edge e=(v,u) of type “source‑to‑proposition”, let the source v have reliability r_v(t)\in[0,1] and the edge have confidence c_e(t)\in[0,1]. Define raw weights \widetilde{W}_{uv}=r_v c_e. For other edges (e.g. implication), similar semantic rules apply. For each proposition i, collect all incoming weights from sources and other propositions, and normalise to obtain the row‑stochastic matrix \mathbf{W}(t):

W_{ij}(t)=\frac{\widetilde{W}_{ij}}{\sum_{k}\widetilde{W}_{ik}},

with the convention 0/0=0 (isolated nodes remain at their current accreditation). The matrix is extended to all proposition nodes by adding self‑loops with small weight if necessary to ensure aperiodicity.

Lemma 8.1. If the underlying information graph G is strongly connected and there exists at least one source with positive reliability, then \mathbf{W}(t) is primitive for every t with positive edge weights. Proof. Strong connectivity guarantees that the adjacency pattern is irreducible; the presence of a self‑loop (or a cycle of length coprime to the graph’s cycle lengths) implies aperiodicity. Since all weights are non‑negative and rows sum to one, the matrix is primitive. ∎

Theorem 8.1 (Consensus of accreditation). Assume \mathbf{W} is constant and primitive. Then the linear dynamical system \mathbf{A}(t+1)=\mathbf{W}\mathbf{A}(t) converges to the consensus vector \bar{A}\mathbf{1} with \bar{A}=\mathbf{v}^\top\mathbf{A}(0), where \mathbf{v} is the left Perron vector of \mathbf{W}. Moreover, \|\mathbf{A}(t)-\bar{A}\mathbf{1}\|_\infty \le C\,|\lambda_2|^t for some C>0.

Proof. Since \mathbf{W} is primitive, it has a simple eigenvalue 1 and all other eigenvalues satisfy |\lambda_j|<1. Write the initial accreditation vector in the eigenbasis: \mathbf{A}(0)=\bar{A}\mathbf{1}+\sum_{j\ge2}c_j\mathbf{u}_j, where \mathbf{W}\mathbf{u}_j=\lambda_j\mathbf{u}_j. Then \mathbf{A}(t)=\bar{A}\mathbf{1}+\sum_{j\ge2}c_j\lambda_j^t\mathbf{u}_j. The dominant term among the transient modes is bounded by |\lambda_2|^t, which gives the exponential convergence. ∎

Corollary 8.1. The mixing time to reach accuracy \varepsilon is t_{\text{mix}}\approx \gamma^{-1}\log\varepsilon^{-1}, where \gamma=1-|\lambda_2| is the spectral gap.

Thus, accreditation on a strongly connected, honest information network naturally tends towards a globally consistent value, with speed dictated by the network’s connectivity.

