Chapter 9: Multi‑Agent Verification and Independence

Theorem 9.1 (Condorcet Jury Theorem under correlation; Becker et al. 2025, Chen & Suen 2025). Suppose n agents each have independent correctness probability p>1/2 but their votes are correlated through a network with average pairwise correlation \bar{\rho}. Then the effective sample size is

n_{\text{eff}}=\frac{n}{1+(n-1)\bar{\rho}},

and the majority accuracy approaches 1 as n_{\text{eff}}\to\infty.

In Accreditation Theory, when combining multiple source assessments, the weight w_j of source j must be deflated by a factor 1/(1+(n-1)\bar{\rho}) to prevent artificial amplification from correlated sources. This directly implements the principle that genuine independent verification contributes far more than coordinated repetition.

