Chapter 11: Toward Multi‑Planetary Byzantine‑Resilient Accreditation

In a fully distributed, multi‑planetary civilisation, accreditation must be computed locally and be robust to Byzantine (arbitrarily faulty or malicious) nodes. A standard PBFT protocol with N\ge 3f+1 nodes can reach consensus on a discrete value. For continuous accreditation, each settlement broadcasts its local bpa; a PBFT‑inspired voting scheme selects a consensus bpa using a robust central tendency (e.g., the median of mass functions). The work of Park & Kim (2026) on Byzantine‑resilient satellite constellations demonstrates the feasibility of such cross‑verification in physically distributed systems with communication delays. The sheaf‑theoretic formulation of Chapter 12.5 further shows that perfect global consensus is not required: local consistency on shared edges suffices.

