Chapter 12: Future Mathematical Development — Formalized

This chapter provides a rigorous development of the open problems, structured as a research programme with precise definitions, proved theorems, and conjectures with explicit proof strategies.

12.1 Axiomatization of the Accreditation Index

Definition 12.1 (Cross‑source aggregation). Let \mathcal{A}: (m_1,\dots,m_n)\mapsto m^* combine bpas from n independent sources into a single bpa over the fixed frame \Omega. We consider the following axioms:

· (P1) Unanimity: \mathcal{A}(m,\dots,m)=m.
· (P2) Coherent marginalisation: For any coarsening \Pi:\Omega\to\Omega', aggregate‑then‑marginalise equals marginalise‑then‑aggregate.
· (P3) External Bayesianity: For any likelihood (conditional bpa) m(\cdot\mid E),

\mathcal{A}\big(m_1(\cdot\mid E),\dots,m_n(\cdot\mid E)\big)=\mathcal{A}(m_1,\dots,m_n)(\cdot\mid E).

· (P4) Independence of irrelevant information: The relative weights assigned to two disjoint events should not depend on the mass given to a third event independent of them.

Theorem 12.1 (Accreditation Impossibility Theorem; Genest 1984, Genest & Zidek 1986). No aggregation operator on probability measures satisfies both (P2) and (P3) except in the degenerate case where all sources agree.

Proof. Linear pooling m^*=\sum w_j m_j satisfies (P2) but fails (P3): updating each source’s probability with a likelihood and then linearly pooling gives a different result from linearly pooling the priors and updating once. Logarithmic pooling m^*\propto\prod m_j^{w_j} is the unique (up to weights) externally Bayesian operator satisfying (P4), but it fails (P2): the log‑pooled probability of a disjoint union is not the sum of the log‑pooled probabilities. An exhaustive analysis (Genest 1984) shows that no continuous, strictly increasing operator can satisfy both. ∎

Consequence for Accreditation. Because evidence and sources arrive asynchronously, the order of operations must be irrelevant. Hence external Bayesianity (P3) is non‑negotiable, and we must sacrifice (P2). The canonical cross‑source pool is therefore the log‑linear pool on commonalities:

Q^*(A) \propto \prod_{j=1}^n Q_j(A)^{w_j},

with w_j proportional to source reliability S_j (Chapter 5). The corresponding bpa is recovered by Möbius inversion. For fusing multiple evidence items about the same proposition from a single source, Dempster’s rule remains the correct operator.

Conjecture 12.1 (Extension to full bpas). The impossibility of jointly satisfying (P2) and (P3) holds for arbitrary (non‑Bayesian) bpas. In particular, the log‑linear pool on commonalities Q^*(A)=\prod Q_j(A)^{w_j} (with normalisation Q^*(\emptyset)=1) commutes with Fagin–Halpern conditioning only if every source’s bpa is Bayesian. Proof strategy. Write the conditional commonality Q(A\mid B)=Q(A\cap B)/Q(B) for B fixed. The requirement \mathcal{A}(Q_1(\cdot\mid B),\dots)= \mathcal{A}(Q_1,\dots)(\cdot\mid B) leads to the functional equation

\prod_j Q_j(A\cap B)^{w_j} \Big/ \prod_j Q_j(B)^{w_j} = \prod_j \left( \frac{Q_j(A\cap B)}{Q_j(B)} \right)^{w_j},

which forces each Q_j to be a product of independent factors over singletons—the defining property of a Bayesian bpa. A full proof requires verifying that the log‑linear pool of commonalities always yields a valid commonality function; this is known to be true for the product of completely monotone functions but needs an explicit check. This lemma would be a genuinely new contribution.

12.2 Convergence of the Continuous‑Time Adaptive Dynamics

When the coefficients \alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta,\epsilon in (10.1) are allowed to adapt based on observed errors, we obtain a two‑timescale stochastic approximation.

Definition 12.2 (Adaptive accreditation). Let \theta=(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta,\epsilon)\in\mathbb{R}^5_+ be the parameter vector. We update \theta slowly:

\theta_{t+1}=\theta_t + a_t \nabla_\theta \ell(\theta_t,\phi_t),

where \ell is a loss function (e.g., prediction error of verification outcomes). The fast variable \phi is the accreditation state \mathbf{A}(t) evolving under the current \theta:

A_i(t+1)=A_i(t)+b_t\big(\alpha E_i+\beta S_i+\gamma C_i-\delta X_i-\epsilon U_i\big)+\sqrt{b_t}\,\sigma\,\eta_t,

with step sizes satisfying b_t/a_t\to0 and \sum a_t=\infty,\sum a_t^2<\infty.

Theorem 12.2 (Borkar 1997). Under Lipschitz gradients and the existence of a unique globally asymptotically stable equilibrium of the fast dynamics for each fixed \theta, the slow parameter \theta_t converges almost surely to a critical point of the averaged loss.

Proof. This is a direct application of Borkar’s two‑timescale theorem. The key assumption—that the fast process has a stationary distribution depending smoothly on \theta—is yet to be verified for network propagation with adaptive weights; verifying this is an open problem. ∎

Open problem. Proving joint convergence requires showing that the fast accreditation dynamics \mathbf{A}(t) is well approximated by a stationary measure that is a smooth function of \theta. This is difficult because the fast process is not a simple gradient flow but a graph diffusion. Techniques from cooperative dynamical systems (Benaïm–Hirsch) may be needed.

12.3 Stability and Adversarial Robustness

Definition 12.3 (Accreditation influence function). For an adversarial perturbation of weight \varepsilon injected at a source, the influence on A_i is

\mathrm{IF}(A_i;x)=\lim_{\varepsilon\to0}\frac{A_i^\varepsilon-A_i}{\varepsilon}.

Theorem 12.3 (Certified robustness via randomized smoothing). Replace the propagation matrix \mathbf{W} with a smoothed version \widetilde{\mathbf{W}} obtained by adding i.i.d. Gaussian noise \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2) to each raw edge weight before row‑normalisation. Then for any adversarial budget of k edge insertions/deletions, the smoothed accreditation \widetilde{A}_i satisfies, with probability \ge 1-\delta,

|\widetilde{A}_i - A_i| \le L\sqrt{\frac{k}{\sigma^2}\log\frac{2n}{\delta}},

where L is the global Lipschitz constant of the function \Phi\circ(network propagation) with respect to the edge weights.

Proof. Gaussian smoothing makes the function f(\mathbf{W})=\mathbf{A} Lipschitz with constant L/\sigma. The \ell_2-norm of the perturbation to the weight vector caused by k edge modifications is at most \sqrt{k}; the standard concentration argument for Gaussian smoothing (Cohen et al. 2019, adapted to regression) then gives the bound. ∎

Lemma 12.1 (Lipschitz constant of \Phi). For the regularised index \Phi(B,P,U)=\frac{B+P}{2}(1-U) with U=\widehat{U}_{\!\epsilon}, the gradient satisfies \|\nabla\Phi\|_2\le \sqrt{0.5^2+0.5^2+1^2}+\|\nabla U_{\!\epsilon}\|_2. Since U_{\!\epsilon} is Lipschitz on the probability simplex with constant L_U= \frac{C|\Omega|}{\epsilon} (for small \epsilon), we obtain an explicit global Lipschitz bound L_\Phi\le 2 for sufficiently large \epsilon. Thus the full pipeline is Lipschitz, enabling certified robustness.

Open problem. The bound relies on a Lipschitz property of the entire network propagation map. Deriving a tight constant directly from network structure (e.g., spectral gap) would yield stronger, architecture‑aware certificates.

12.4 Information‑Theoretic Bounds — an Accreditation Speed Limit

We now ground the central claim that accreditation cannot grow faster than evidence allows.

Definition 12.4 (Knowledge state process). Let X_t be a Markov process representing the complete state of all bpas and evidence at time t. The accreditation index A_i(t) is a function f_i(X_t). The process X_t generates a time‑dependent density p_t(x).

Theorem 12.4 (Speed limit for accreditation). For any proposition i,

\left|\frac{d}{dt}\mathbb{E}[A_i]\right| \le \sigma_{A_i}(t)\sqrt{I_F(t)},

where \sigma_{A_i}(t) is the standard deviation of A_i under p_t, and I_F(t) is the temporal Fisher information of the knowledge state.

Proof. Since A_i is a function of the Markov process X_t, this is a direct instance of the Ito–Dechant thermodynamic speed limit (Theorem 2.3). ∎

Conjecture 12.2 (Extension to cumulative accreditation). The cumulative accreditation T_i(t)=\int_0^t A_i(\tau)d\tau is a path‑dependent functional. We hypothesise a speed limit in terms of path‑space Fisher information:

\left|\frac{d}{dt}\langle T_i\rangle\right| \le \sigma_{T_i}(t)\sqrt{I_F^{\mathrm{path}}(t)},

where I_F^{\mathrm{path}}(t)=\mathbb{E}[(\partial_t\ln P_{[0,t]})^2]. Proof strategy. Express T_i as an additive functional of a semi‑Markov process and use the Mori–Zwanzig formalism to derive a generalised uncertainty relation. The difficulty lies in bounding the memory kernel in terms of the path‑space Fisher information. This would establish a deep link between accreditation dynamics and stochastic thermodynamics.

12.5 Sheaf‑Theoretic Accreditation

Definition 12.5 (Accreditation sheaf). Over the directed information graph G=(V,E), define a cellular sheaf \mathcal{F} as follows: for each proposition vertex v, \mathcal{F}(v)=\mathbb{R}^2, with coordinates (B_v,P_v) (belief and plausibility). For each edge e=(v,u) of type “cites” (source v to proposition u), let \mathcal{F}(e)=\mathbb{R}, and the restriction map \mathcal{F}_{v\triangleleft e}:\mathcal{F}(v)\to\mathbb{R} extracts the reliability‑weighted average, while \mathcal{F}_{u\triangleleft e} projects the accreditation index of u. Other edge types have similarly defined maps.

Definition 12.6 (Sheaf Laplacian). With the coboundary operator \delta as in §2.5, the sheaf Laplacian L_{\mathcal{F}}=\delta^*\delta is a symmetric positive semidefinite operator on the space of cochains. The dynamics

\frac{d\mathbf{A}}{dt} = -L_{\mathcal{F}}\mathbf{A}

drive the system towards harmonic cochains.

Theorem 12.5 (Harmonic accreditation). For the sheaf \mathcal{F} defined above, the continuous‑time dynamics converge to \ker L_{\mathcal{F}}. A cochain is harmonic iff for every edge e=(v,u), the accreditation values satisfy the local consistency condition \mathcal{F}_{v\triangleleft e}(\mathbf{A}_v)=\mathcal{F}_{u\triangleleft e}(\mathbf{A}_u). The cohomology group H^1(G;\mathcal{F}) measures the dimension of the space of irreducible inconsistencies that no amount of communication can resolve.

Proof. Since L_{\mathcal{F}} is symmetric positive semidefinite, the standard spectral decomposition gives exponential convergence to the kernel. The characterisation of harmonic cochains follows directly from (\delta\mathbf{A})_e=0. The cohomological interpretation is a standard fact of cellular sheaf theory (Hansen & Ghrist 2021). ∎

Multi‑planetary interpretation. A Martian settlement does not need to possess the identical accreditation value as Earth; it only needs to satisfy the local consistency equations on the edges that connect them. The obstruction to full global consensus is exactly the non‑trivial cohomology H^1(G;\mathcal{F}), which may be unavoidable due to topology or adversarial interference. This reframes accreditation as a cohomological problem—a perspective unique to this thesis.

Conjecture 12.5 (Time‑varying sheaves). When the restriction maps change over time (e.g., due to varying source reliability), the sheaf becomes time‑dependent. Under slow variation and uniform boundedness of the Laplacian’s spectral gap away from zero, one expects the solution to track the instantaneous harmonic subspace. Proving this formally requires extending Hansen–Ghrist theory to non‑autonomous linear systems on sheaves.

12.6 Distributed Algorithms — Sheaf‑ADMM Federated Accreditation

Definition 12.7 (Sheaf‑ADMM). Consider the global optimisation problem

\min_{x_v\in\mathcal{F}(v)} \sum_v f_v(x_v) \quad \text{s.t. } \mathcal{F}_{v\triangleleft e}(x_v)=\mathcal{F}_{u\triangleleft e}(x_u)\ \forall e=(v,u),

where f_v is a local cost (e.g., a regulariser based on private evidence). ADMM decouples this with auxiliary variables z_e and updates in parallel across nodes. The standard convergence theory (Boyd et al. 2011) guarantees linear convergence under strong convexity.

Algorithm 12.1 (Differentially Private Sheaf‑ADMM). At each ADMM iteration, before sharing the variable z_{v,e} with neighbours, node v adds Laplace noise with scale \Delta/\epsilon, where \Delta is the sensitivity of the update. This gives per‑iteration \epsilon-DP. After K iterations, basic composition yields K\epsilon-DP.

Conjecture 12.6 (Convergence of noisy Sheaf‑ADMM). Under standard convexity and smoothness assumptions, noisy Sheaf‑ADMM converges to a neighbourhood of the sheaf‑harmonic solution, with size O(\sigma^2/\rho), where \sigma^2 is the noise variance and \rho the ADMM penalty parameter. Proof strategy. The noise acts as a bounded perturbation to the ADMM operator; using the variational inequality characterisation of ADMM, one can bound the distance to the true solution by a function of the cumulative noise. The result follows from the contraction property of the noiseless ADMM.

Connection to JOUL. The author’s prior work on JOUL solved an energy‑balance constrained distributed optimisation under differential privacy. That problem has the identical mathematical structure, with the linear constraint A x = b. Here the sheaf‑consistency constraint \delta x=0 is also a linear system. Thus the same algorithmic template and privacy analysis transfer directly; the present thesis completes the formal specification for the information accreditation domain.

