Appendix E: Thermodynamic Speed Limit (Ito–Dechant Theorem 2.3)

Theorem E.1 (Ito & Dechant 2020). Let X_t be a time‑homogeneous Markov process with probability density p_t(x) evolving under the Fokker–Planck equation. For any observable O(x) with finite variance \sigma_O^2(t) = \mathbb{E}_{p_t}[O^2] - (\mathbb{E}_{p_t}[O])^2,

\left| \frac{d}{dt}\mathbb{E}_{p_t}[O] \right| \le \sigma_O(t)\sqrt{I_F(t)},

where I_F(t) = \mathbb{E}_{p_t}[(\partial_t\ln p_t)^2] is the temporal Fisher information.

Proof sketch. The time derivative of the expectation is

\frac{d}{dt}\mathbb{E}[O] = \int O(x)\,\partial_t p_t(x)\,dx = \int O(x)\,(\partial_t\ln p_t(x))\,p_t(x)\,dx = \mathbb{E}[O\cdot \partial_t\ln p_t].

By the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality,

\left|\mathbb{E}[O\cdot \partial_t\ln p_t]\right| \le \sqrt{\mathbb{E}[O^2]\,\mathbb{E}[(\partial_t\ln p_t)^2]}.

However, we want a bound involving the centered observable \bar{O}=O-\mathbb{E}[O]. Because \mathbb{E}[\partial_t\ln p_t]=0 (probability conservation), we have \mathbb{E}[O\cdot\partial_t\ln p_t] = \mathbb{E}[\bar{O}\cdot\partial_t\ln p_t]. Then

|\frac{d}{dt}\mathbb{E}[O]| \le \sqrt{\mathbb{E}[\bar{O}^2]\,\mathbb{E}[(\partial_t\ln p_t)^2]} = \sigma_O(t)\sqrt{I_F(t)}.

This inequality is tight for some observables. For the full derivation including the conditions on the Markov generator, see Ito & Dechant (2020). ∎

We apply this with O=A_i and p_t the distribution of the knowledge state to obtain Theorem 12.4.

