Appendix F: Sheaf Laplacian and Harmonic Cochains (Proof of Theorem 2.2/12.5)

Definition F.1 (Sheaf Laplacian). For a cellular sheaf \mathcal{F} on a graph G=(V,E) with restriction maps \mathcal{F}_{v\triangleleft e}, the coboundary operator \delta: C^0(G;\mathcal{F})\to C^1(G;\mathcal{F}) is

(\delta x)_e = \mathcal{F}_{v\triangleleft e}(x_v) - \mathcal{F}_{u\triangleleft e}(x_u), \quad e=(u,v).

The adjoint \delta^*: C^1(G;\mathcal{F})\to C^0(G;\mathcal{F}) with respect to the natural inner products is

(\delta^* y)_v = \sum_{e\ni v} \mathcal{F}_{v\triangleleft e}^* (y_e),

where the orientation is taken from v to the other endpoint with a sign. The sheaf Laplacian is L_{\mathcal{F}} = \delta^*\delta.

Theorem F.1 (Harmonic convergence). The matrix L_{\mathcal{F}} is symmetric positive semidefinite. The dynamical system \dot{x} = -L_{\mathcal{F}} x converges to the subspace \ker L_{\mathcal{F}}, which consists exactly of cochains x satisfying (\delta x)_e=0 for all edges e (harmonic cochains). The dimension of the kernel equals the dimension of the zeroth sheaf cohomology H^0(G;\mathcal{F}); the first cohomology H^1(G;\mathcal{F}) measures the obstruction to the existence of a global section.

Proof. Symmetry and positivity follow from the definition L_{\mathcal{F}}=\delta^*\delta. The quadratic form \langle x, L_{\mathcal{F}} x\rangle = \|\delta x\|^2. Therefore, L_{\mathcal{F}} x=0 iff \delta x=0. For the linear ODE, the solution can be written as x(t) = e^{-tL_{\mathcal{F}}}x(0). Decompose x(0) = x_{\text{harm}} + x_{\perp} with x_{\text{harm}}\in\ker L_{\mathcal{F}} and x_{\perp} in the orthogonal complement (which is the span of eigenvectors with positive eigenvalues). Then x(t) = x_{\text{harm}} + e^{-tL_{\mathcal{F}}}x_{\perp}. The exponential term dies out as t\to\infty because the smallest positive eigenvalue \lambda_{\min}>0 gives decay at rate e^{-\lambda_{\min}t}. Thus x(t)\to x_{\text{harm}}.

The cohomological interpretation: the kernel of L_{\mathcal{F}} is H^0(G;\mathcal{F}) (global sections). The obstruction to a global consistent assignment of accreditation—i.e., the inconsistency that cannot be resolved—is given by the cohomology group H^1(G;\mathcal{F}), which is the quotient \ker\delta/\operatorname{im}\delta. In the accreditation context, this measures the residual disagreement in the network even after full diffusion. ∎

