Appendix H: Two‑Timescale Stochastic Approximation (Borkar’s Theorem)

Theorem H.1 (Borkar 1997). Consider the coupled iterations

\theta_{t+1} = \theta_t + a_t h(\theta_t,\phi_t) + a_t M_{t+1}^{(1)},

\phi_{t+1} = \phi_t + b_t g(\theta_t,\phi_t) + b_t M_{t+1}^{(2)},

where a_t,b_t are positive step‑size sequences satisfying \sum_t a_t = \sum_t b_t = \infty, \sum_t (a_t^2+b_t^2)<\infty, and b_t/a_t \to 0. The functions h,g are Lipschitz, and the martingale difference sequences M^{(i)} have bounded second moments. Assume that for each fixed \theta, the ODE \dot{\phi} = g(\theta,\phi) has a unique globally asymptotically stable equilibrium \phi^*(\theta) and the map \theta\mapsto \phi^*(\theta) is Lipschitz. Then \theta_t converges almost surely to a compact connected internally chain transitive set of the ODE \dot{\theta} = h(\theta,\phi^*(\theta)).

In the setting of adaptive accreditation, the fast process is the accreditation state \mathbf{A} under fixed parameters, and the slow process adjusts \alpha,\beta,\dots. The theorem provides convergence under the stated assumptions, which remain to be verified. The main open challenge is establishing the global asymptotic stability of the fast network diffusion and its smooth dependence on \theta.

