Appendix J: Lyapunov–Krasovskii Stability for Delayed Consensus (Sketch of Theorem 10.1)

Theorem 10.1 (Stability under fixed delay). Consider the linear delayed system

\dot{\mathbf{A}}(t) = \mathbf{M}_0\mathbf{A}(t) + \mathbf{M}_\tau \mathbf{A}(t-\tau),

where \mathbf{M}_0 is a matrix representing the instantaneous consensus dynamics (typically -L_{\mathcal{F}}) and \mathbf{M}_\tau represents the delayed interactions. Assume the zero‑delay system \dot{\mathbf{A}} = (\mathbf{M}_0+\mathbf{M}_\tau)\mathbf{A} is exponentially stable with decay rate \gamma. Then there exists a threshold \tau^* = \frac{\pi}{2\|\mathbf{M}_\tau\|} (in the spectral norm) such that for \tau<\tau^*, the delayed system remains exponentially stable.

Proof sketch. Define a Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional

V(t) = \mathbf{A}(t)^{\top} P \mathbf{A}(t) + \int_{t-\tau}^{t} \mathbf{A}(s)^{\top} Q \mathbf{A}(s)\,ds,

where P is the positive definite solution to the Lyapunov equation for the zero‑delay system, and Q is a chosen positive definite matrix. Computing the derivative along trajectories yields

\dot{V}(t) = 2\mathbf{A}(t)^{\top}P[\mathbf{M}_0\mathbf{A}(t)+\mathbf{M}_\tau\mathbf{A}(t-\tau)] + \mathbf{A}(t)^{\top}Q\mathbf{A}(t) - \mathbf{A}(t-\tau)^{\top}Q\mathbf{A}(t-\tau).

Using the inequality 2\mathbf{A}(t)^{\top}P\mathbf{M}_\tau\mathbf{A}(t-\tau) \le \mathbf{A}(t)^{\top}P\mathbf{M}_\tau Q^{-1}\mathbf{M}_\tau^{\top}P\mathbf{A}(t) + \mathbf{A}(t-\tau)^{\top}Q\mathbf{A}(t-\tau) (Young’s inequality), we can cancel the Q terms and bound \dot{V}(t) \le \mathbf{A}(t)^{\top}[P\mathbf{M}_0+\mathbf{M}_0^{\top}P + P\mathbf{M}_\tau Q^{-1}\mathbf{M}_\tau^{\top}P + Q]\mathbf{A}(t). By choosing Q=\epsilon I and using the fact that the zero‑delay system is stable, one can show the existence of \tau^* such that the bracket is negative definite for \tau<\tau^*. The threshold is proportional to \|\mathbf{M}_\tau\|^{-1} and the stability margin of the zero‑delay system. The precise value \tau^* = \pi/(2\|\mathbf{M}_\tau\|) comes from a frequency‑domain argument (Nyquist criterion) for a scalar delay system; for the matrix case, the condition is similar in terms of the maximum singular value. ∎

This theorem provides the rigorous guarantee that interplanetary accreditation remains stable under bounded communication delays, provided the network’s consensus strength is sufficiently high.


